---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const projectCount = (await getCollection('projects')).length;
---

<Base
  title="Start here: what I actually do (in plain words)"
  description="A plain-English explanation of what Madhan Kumar Reddy does: builds private AI systems that run inside your own office so your confidential data never goes to the internet. No jargon."
>
  <h1>Start here</h1>
  <p style="font-size:1.15rem; color:var(--fg)">
    Three kinds of people land on this site. Pick your door, or scroll on for
    the plain-English version, no jargon, I promise.
  </p>

  <div class="router">
    <article class="card">
      <h3>You're hiring</h3>
      <p class="oneliner">Recruiter, hiring manager, or team lead sizing me up for a role.</p>
      <p>Start with the <a href="/resume-2page.pdf">2-page résumé (PDF)</a>, or the <a href="/resume">full résumé</a> with every project linked. For proof, the <a href="/">homepage</a> lets you load each system onto my GPU and see what it measured, and <a href="/projects">projects</a> lists all {projectCount}, statuses honest.</p>
      <p class="meta"><a href="/resume-2page.pdf">2-page PDF →</a> <a href="/resume">full résumé →</a></p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>You have a problem to solve</h3>
      <p class="oneliner">You need private AI in your business, or local video, voice and 3D work without per-seat cloud bills.</p>
      <p>See <a href="/services">what I offer and what it costs</a>: deployment, audit, creative pipelines, retainer, prices listed. The one-page <a href="#briefs">briefs (PDF)</a> below are made for forwarding to whoever signs off.</p>
      <p class="meta"><a href="/services">services + pricing →</a></p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>You're an engineer</h3>
      <p class="oneliner">You want the numbers, the failures, and the configs.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/calculator">interactive field guide</a> (VRAM math, throughput knees, break-even), the <a href="/projects">projects</a> with public repos, and the <a href="/blog">failure log</a>, dead ends written up because they were the expensive part.</p>
      <p class="meta"><a href="/calculator">open the field guide →</a></p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h2>What I do, in one sentence</h2>
  <p style="font-size:1.1rem">
    <strong>I set up AI systems that run inside your own office, on your own
    computer, so your private information never gets sent over the internet to
    a company like ChatGPT.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>Why that matters</h2>
  <p>
    Most AI tools (ChatGPT and the like) work by sending your text, documents,
    or images to a company's servers somewhere else. For a lot of people that's
    fine. But if you're a <strong>law firm, a clinic, an accountant, or any
    business with confidential files</strong>, sending that information to an
    outside company can break your rules, or just feel wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    I build the same kind of AI (a chatbot, a document searcher, an assistant),
    but it lives <strong>entirely on a computer you own</strong>. Your files
    never leave the building. That's the whole idea. People call it "private AI"
    or "on-premises AI." I call it the obvious choice for anyone handling
    sensitive work.
  </p>

  <h2>What I can build for you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A private ChatGPT</strong> for your team that can answer questions, draft documents, and summarise files, and never sends a word outside your office.</li>
    <li><strong>A private document search for your firm</strong>: ask "which contracts let the other side terminate early?" and get the answer with the exact clause cited. When your documents don't say, it tells you so instead of guessing. Every search is logged, and staff only see the matters they're cleared for.</li>
    <li><strong>Video, voice and image tools</strong>: remove an object from a video, turn a comic page into a narrated video, create a talking avatar from a photo and a voice sample. All on your own machine.</li>
    <li><strong>3D models of your products from a photo</strong>, ready for an online shop, with an automatic check that rejects any model that doesn't meet the marketplace's rules.</li>
    <li><strong>A health-check service</strong>: once it's set up, I keep it running, backed up, and updated so you don't have to think about it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Proof, in plain words</h2>
  <p>Three things I built and measured, each with the full story one click away:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The legal search that refuses to guess.</strong> Tested on 150 questions over real contracts, it finds the right clause first noticeably more often than the usual "chat with your PDFs" approach, and it cites where every answer came from. <a href="/projects/sovereign-legal-rag">The results →</a></li>
    <li><strong>One graphics card, eight people at once.</strong> I measured how many people a single high-end card can serve before they start waiting: eight working at the same time, then the rest queue. That's the number you need to size a machine for your team. <a href="/blog/one-rtx-5090-how-many-users-the-honest-answer">The test →</a></li>
    <li><strong>The checker that stopped a bad product model.</strong> Of five real products turned into 3D models, three passed, one was refused before it could reach a shop listing, and one was flagged because it came out 703 mm wide instead of 650 mm. <a href="/case-studies-product-photo-to-3d/">The case study →</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why me</h2>
  <p>
    I'm honest about how I work: I don't write software by hand in the
    traditional way. I design the system, direct AI tools to build it, and
    then carefully check and operate it. I've spent years in
    <strong>quality control and data security</strong> (the careful,
    rule-following kind of work), which is exactly what you want from someone
    handling your confidential setup.
  </p>
  <p>
    And I prove everything in public: <strong>{projectCount} projects</strong>
    documented, <strong>13 fixes sent to widely used AI tools, 5 accepted</strong>
    by their maintainers, and the mistakes written up next to the wins. You can
    see all of it before you ever pay me.
  </p>

  <h2>What I won't promise</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Broadcast-grade avatars.</strong> My local talking avatars are good enough for social media. Close-ups for TV still need cloud tools, and I'll say so before you pay.</li>
    <li><strong>A private model that matches the biggest cloud models.</strong> A model that fits on your own machine is very capable, but it is not the largest one in the world. Sensitive work stays local; anything else goes to the cloud only if your rules allow it.</li>
    <li><strong>Every video edit.</strong> Glass, liquids and very dark footage can still beat the tools. Hard footage gets a free sample frame first, so you see the result before you commit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to start</h2>
  <p>
    The easiest first step is a <strong>short paid audit</strong>: I look at what
    you have, what you want, and what's possible, then give you a plain written
    report. No commitment beyond that.
  </p>
  <p style="font-size:1.05rem">
    👉 <a href="/services">See what I offer and what it costs</a><br />
    👉 <a href="/blog#case-studies">See real examples</a><br />
    👉 Or just email me: <a href="mailto:reddy.madhankumar.sg@gmail.com">reddy.madhankumar.sg@gmail.com</a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="briefs">The one-page briefs</h2>
  <p>
    Four of the systems I operate, each as a downloadable one-page PDF: what
    it does, the measured numbers, and what it honestly can't do yet. Made for
    forwarding to a colleague or a boss:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/briefs/sovereign-stack-brief.pdf">Sovereign AI Stack</a>: a private LLM that physically can't leak (PDF)</li>
    <li><a href="/briefs/legal-rag-brief.pdf">Sovereign Legal RAG</a>: private document search that cites the clause and refuses to guess (PDF)</li>
    <li><a href="/briefs/creative-stack-brief.pdf">Creative Stack</a>: local video generation, object removal, 4K finishing (PDF)</li>
    <li><a href="/briefs/audio-stack-brief.pdf">Audio Stack</a>: voice cloning, avatars, music, dubbing (PDF)</li>
  </ul>

  <style>
    .router { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 0.9rem; margin: 1.6rem 0 0.6rem; }
    .router .card { margin: 0; }
    .router h3 { margin-top: 0; }
    .router .meta { gap: 0.9rem; }
    @media (max-width: 44rem) { .router { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  </style>

  <p class="meta" style="margin-top:2rem">
    Based in Singapore · working remotely with international clients · on-site
    travel available for overseas engagements · the technical deep-dives (for
    the engineers) are in <a href="/projects">projects</a> and
    <a href="/blog">the blog</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
